      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© The Author(s) 2015.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "In cases of otalgia without any accompanying findings, some patients locate their otalgia below the attachment of the lobule, at the apex of the jugulodigastric region."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "PURPOSE",
            "content": "To present a series of these patients for whom nasal steroids or myringotomy usually ameliorated their pain."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "Thirty-two patients with normal physical examinations, tympanograms, and age-appropriate audiograms spontaneously indicated otalgia at \"the otalgia point.\" Inspection of the oropharynx and nasal airway, palpation of the neck and temporal mandibular joints, and nasopharyngoscopy/laryngoscopy ruled out referred causes of otalgia. Patients were offered either nasal steroid spray or trial myringotomy followed by tympanostomy tube."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Otalgia improved in all 10 (100%) patients who selected nasal steroids. Otalgia resolved in 17 of 20 (85%) myringotomy participants. Three patients declined intervention. In all, symptoms improved in 27/29 treated patients (93%)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "This description of \"the otalgia point\" introduces a new otolaryngologic gesture in physical examination that can aid in the therapeutic management of some patients with otalgia and normal examinations. This is an uncontrolled case series that serves as a pilot study for further exploration of this gesture."
          }
        ]
      },
